<div id="alg3.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><math id="alg3.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}=\exp(\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}-\mathbf{Lse}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐋𝐨𝐠𝐢𝐭𝐬</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐋𝐨𝐠𝐢𝐭𝐬</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐋𝐬𝐞</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}=\exp(\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}-\mathbf{Lse}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">;
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><math id="alg3.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Index}_{j}=[jB_{v}+1,\cdots,(j+1)B_{v}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐈𝐧𝐝𝐞𝐱</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Index}_{j}=[jB_{v}+1,\cdots,(j+1)B_{v}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">;
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><math id="alg3.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐄</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> if </span><math id="alg3.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Index}_{j}=\mathbf{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐈𝐧𝐝𝐞𝐱</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Index}_{j}=\mathbf{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, otherwise </span><math id="alg3.l11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">; {</span><math id="alg3.l11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}\ \in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐄</mi><mo lspace="0.708em" mathsize="0.800em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}\ \in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l11.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><math id="alg3.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathbf{H}_{i}\mathrel{{+}{=}}(\nabla\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}+\mathbf{E})\mathbf{W}_{j}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐇</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐋𝐨𝐠𝐢𝐭𝐬</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐄</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathbf{H}_{i}\mathrel{{+}{=}}(\nabla\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}+\mathbf{E})\mathbf{W}_{j}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><math id="alg3.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathbf{W}_{j}\mathrel{{+}{=}}(\nabla\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}+\mathbf{E})^{\top}\mathbf{H}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐋𝐨𝐠𝐢𝐭𝐬</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐄</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐇</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathbf{W}_{j}\mathrel{{+}{=}}(\nabla\mathbf{Logits}_{ij}+\mathbf{E})^{\top}\mathbf{H}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Sequence-level fusion of LM head and loss function</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Sparse Attention Integration</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss how BurstAttention integrates with sparse
attention patterns, including common causal attention masking and other block-wise sparse attention masking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Causal Attention.</span>
In the training process of most LLMs and LMMs, the goal is to predict the next token in the sequence.
In this task, the training objective is to maximize the log-likelihood of the next token given the previous tokens. To accomplish this, Transformer-based models commonly employ causal attention masking. For each token in the sequence, causal attention masking makes the token only attend to the key-value pairs of its preceding tokens. We can formulate this causal attention pattern as</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{O}_{i}=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}[\mathbf{K}_{1},\mathbf{K}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{K}_{i}]^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)[\mathbf{V}_{1},\mathbf{V}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{O}_{i}=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}[\mathbf{K}_{1},\mathbf{K}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{K}_{i}]^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)[\mathbf{V}_{1},\mathbf{V}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the query embedding of the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{j},\mathbf{V}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{j},\mathbf{V}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the key and value embeddings of the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th token.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To balance the workload of causal attention in Context Parallelism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, two typical methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brandon et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuzilin, 2023</a>)</cite> have been proposed: striped-way workload balance and zigzag-way workload balance, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 3.4. Sparse Attention Integration ‣ 3. Overall Framework of BurstEngine ‣ BurstEngine: an Efficient Distributed Framework for Training Transformers on Extremely Long Sequences of over 1M Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Next, we will introduce how BurstAttention integrates these two kinds of methods to balance workloads.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.19836v1/lm_head.png" id="S3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/127;" width="238" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9. </span>The illustration of the sequence-level fusion of the LM head and loss function.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For zigzag-way workload balance, the input sequence is initially partitioned along the sequence dimension across multiple devices. Given a sequence containing <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> tokens <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> devices, the sequence is divided into <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2G</annotation></semantics></math> subsequences, where the subsequence length is <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\frac{N}{2G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\frac{N}{2G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th device (<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq G</annotation></semantics></math>), it obtains two subsequences, one in the front and one in the back: <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S^{1}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S^{1}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\{x_{k}\mid k\in[(i-1)\times P+1,i\times P]\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\{x_{k}\mid k\in[(i-1)\times P+1,i\times P]\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S^{2}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S^{2}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\{x_{k}\mid k\in[n-i\times P+1,n-(i-1)\times P]\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\{x_{k}\mid k\in[n-i\times P+1,n-(i-1)\times P]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">After getting subsequences, each device first performs causal attention on the
two subsequences and then communicates with other devices to obtain the key-value.
Based on the partition, the <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th device has a front query and a back query, after getting the front key-value and back key-value from other devices. When the <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th device has the query embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{Q}^{1}_{i},\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{Q}^{1}_{i},\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}_{i},S^{2}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}_{i},S^{2}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and receives <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{K}^{2}_{j}\},\{\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{2}_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{K}^{2}_{j}\},\{\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{2}_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th device’s <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}_{j},S^{2}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}_{j},S^{2}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{O}_{ij}=\begin{cases}\text{CausalATTN}(\{\mathbf{Q}^{1}_{i},\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i}\},\{\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{K}^{2}_{j}\},\{\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{2}_{j}\})&amp;\text{if }i=j,\\
\text{FullATTN}(\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i},\{\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{K}^{2}_{j}\},\{\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{2}_{j}\})&amp;\text{if }i&lt;j,\\
\text{FullATTN}(\{\mathbf{Q}^{1}_{i},\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i}\},\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j})&amp;\text{if }i&gt;j.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>CausalATTN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>FullATTN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>FullATTN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{O}_{ij}=\begin{cases}\text{CausalATTN}(\{\mathbf{Q}^{1}_{i},\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i}\},\{\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{K}^{2}_{j}\},\{\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{2}_{j}\})&amp;\text{if }i=j,\\
\text{FullATTN}(\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i},\{\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{K}^{2}_{j}\},\{\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{2}_{j}\})&amp;\text{if }i&lt;j,\\
\text{FullATTN}(\{\mathbf{Q}^{1}_{i},\mathbf{Q}^{2}_{i}\},\mathbf{K}^{1}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{1}_{j})&amp;\text{if }i&gt;j.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">For striped-way workload balance, the input sequence is initially partitioned along the sequence dimension across multiple devices into <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> subsequences, where the subsequence length is <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\frac{N}{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\frac{N}{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
For the <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th device (<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq G</annotation></semantics></math>), it obtains one subsequence as</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=\left\{x_{k}\mid k\in\left\{i+G\times m\mid m\in[0,P-1]\right\}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=\left\{x_{k}\mid k\in\left\{i+G\times m\mid m\in[0,P-1]\right\}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The striped-way workload balance method ensures that each device can perform causal attention on the same number of tokens. When the <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th device has the query embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and receives the key-value embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{j},\mathbf{V}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{j},\mathbf{V}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th device’s <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can get</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{\mathbf{Q}_{ik}\mid k\in[2,P]\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{\mathbf{Q}_{ik}\mid k\in[2,P]\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E14Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{\mathbf{K}_{ik}\mid k\in[1,P-1]\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{\mathbf{K}_{ik}\mid k\in[1,P-1]\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E14Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{V}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{V}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{\mathbf{V}_{ik}\mid k\in[1,P-1]\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{\mathbf{V}_{ik}\mid k\in[1,P-1]\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E14Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{O}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{O}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\text{CausalATTN}(\mathbf{Q}_{i},\mathbf{K}_{j},\mathbf{V}_{j})&amp;\text{if }i&gt;=j,\\
\text{CausalATTN}(\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i},\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{\prime}_{j})&amp;\text{if }i&lt;j.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>CausalATTN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>CausalATTN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\text{CausalATTN}(\mathbf{Q}_{i},\mathbf{K}_{j},\mathbf{V}_{j})&amp;\text{if }i&gt;=j,\\
\text{CausalATTN}(\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i},\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{j},\mathbf{V}^{\prime}_{j})&amp;\text{if }i&lt;j.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By adopting a zigzag-way workload balance or striped-way workload balance, each device has exactly the same compute workload, and FlashAttention’s optimization of causal attention can be further leveraged to achieve workload balance at the streaming processor level. For BurstEngine, both zigzag-way workload balance and striped-way workload balance can be integrated. From our pilot experiments, integrating BurstEngine and striped-way workload balance achieves better performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19836v1/causal_workload.svg" id="S3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/64;" width="181" height="64"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10. </span>Two types of workload balance methods for distributed causal attention.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19836v1/sparse_workload.svg" id="S3.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/88;" width="181" height="88"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11. </span>Workload balance for block-wise sparse attention.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Block-wise Sparse Attention.</span>
In addition to causal attention, block-wise sparse attention is another common sparse attention pattern. In block-wise sparse attention, the input sequence is divided into blocks, and each token only attends to tokens within the same block or some neighboring blocks. This pattern is widely used in reducing the computation and memory consumption of training Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gray et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gale et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. While sparse patterns are inherently difficult to integrate with conventional Context Parallelism methods due to extreme workload imbalance among workers, BurstEngine adopts a strategy similar to striped-way workload balance to balance the workload for block-wise sparse attention. In block-wise sparse attention, we first divide the sequence into blocks along the sequence dimension, and the size of each block <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{N}_{{\text{blk}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext><mtext>blk</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{N}_{{\text{blk}}}</annotation></semantics></math> needs to be a multiple of the number of devices <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a strict requirement for block-wise sparse attention workload balance. Then we define the block-masking matrix as <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{M}_{\text{blk}}\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{\textbf{N}}{\textbf{N}_{\text{blk}}}\times\frac{\textbf{N}}{\textbf{N}_{\text{blk}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">M</mtext><mtext>blk</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mfrac><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext><mtext>blk</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext><mtext>blk</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{M}_{\text{blk}}\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{\textbf{N}}{\textbf{N}_{\text{blk}}}\times\frac{\textbf{N}}{\textbf{N}_{\text{blk}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{M}_{\text{blk}}[i,j]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">M</mtext><mtext>blk</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{M}_{\text{blk}}[i,j]=1</annotation></semantics></math> if all tokens in the <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th block can attend to the tokens in the <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th block.
Then, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 3.4. Sparse Attention Integration ‣ 3. Overall Framework of BurstEngine ‣ BurstEngine: an Efficient Distributed Framework for Training Transformers on Extremely Long Sequences of over 1M Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, we adopt a strategy similar to striped-way workload balance for block-wise sparse attention. With the strategy, each device can get exactly the same compute workload, and there is no unnecessary idle time for each device since the workload is balanced.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Hardware Settings.</span> We adopt two configurations: 32<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800 and 64<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800. For all configurations, each node is equipped with 8<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800-SXM4-80GB GPUs linked via 400GB/s NVLink, 8 NVIDIA Mellanox HDR InfiniBand NICs(200Gb/s), and 128 CPU cores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Model Sizes.</span> In experiments, we mainly perform experiments on two settings of model sizes: LLaMA Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite> with 7 billion parameters (7B, 32 layers, 32 heads, 4096 dimensions, 32K vocabulary tokens) and with 14 billion parameters (14B, 40 layers, 40 heads, 5120 dimensions, 120K vocabulary tokens).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Training Settings.</span> We adopt fully sharded data parallelism (FSDP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajbhandari et al., 2020</a>)</cite> to partition model parameters across devices. All evaluations are conducted with gradient checkpointing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2016</a>)</cite> to achieve better training efficiency under limited memory. For 7B and 14B models, we respectively set the
sequence length to 2M and 1M on 32<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800, and to 4M and 2M on 64<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Implementation details</span> For BurstEngine, we adopt the FSDP implementation from BMTrain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenBMB, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which achieves overlap of communication and computation at the Transformer-block level and supports optimizer offloading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Our Topology-aware ring communication is built on top of NCCL and uses multi-stream programming to overlap inter-node communication, intra-node
communication and computation. Additionally, we incorporate sequence-level fusion of LM head and loss fusion to reduce the memory consumption of storing the outputs of the LM head.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Metrics.</span> For evaluation of training efficiency, we employ tokens per second per GPU (TGS) and model FLOPs utilization (MFU). TGS provides a direct measure of end-to-end training throughput, while MFU reflects the actual utilization of the GPU device. To evaluate memory consumption, we measure the peak memory allocated on each GPU for each method, since peak memory can directly evaluate the scalability of the method to larger models or longer sequences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19836v1/main_exp.svg" id="S4.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:205/187;" width="205" height="187"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12. </span>
The end-to-end training throughput (TGS) of BurstEngine and other baselines.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19836v1/main_exp_mem.svg" id="S4.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:205/172;" width="205" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13. </span>
The peak memory usage (GB) per GPU in the end-to-end training of
BurstEngine and other baselines. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Baselines.</span>
We compare BurstEngine with the following baselines in our experiments:
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Megatron Context Parallelism (CP)</span>: Megatron-LM’s implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA, 2023</a>)</cite> for context parallelism, using RingAttention integrated with zigzag-way workload balance. (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSpeed-Ulysses</span>: DeepSpeed’s implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacobs et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for head parallelism. (3) <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoongTrain-DoubleRing</span>: LoongTrain’s implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for context parallelism, using DoubleRingAttention integrated with zigzag-way workload balance.
(4) <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoongTrain-USP</span>: LoongTrain’s implementation for
Head-Context Hybrid Parallelism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang and Zhao, 2024</a>)</cite>, achieving the state-of-the-art performance for long-sequence Transformer training, adopts head-first device placement and RingAttention with zigzag-way workload balance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Training Performance</h3>

<figure id="S4.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19836v1/kernel_benchmark.svg" id="S4.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:205/167;" width="205" height="167"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14. </span>Performance of different distributed attention implementations </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Throughput Performance.</span> For comparisons of training efficiency, we evaluate the end-to-end training throughput of four baselines and BurstEngine on 7B and 14B models. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 4.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ BurstEngine: an Efficient Distributed Framework for Training Transformers on Extremely Long Sequences of over 1M Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, BurstEngine outperforms all baselines in terms of TGS and MFU on both 7B and 14B models. BurstEngine achieves up to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.19\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.19</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.19\times</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.15\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.15\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedups on 7B/14B models, respectively, on 32<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800 GPUs, compared to the state-of-the-art method LoongTrain-USP. In the 7B/14B model training under the 32<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800 setting, Megatron-CP fails because of the out-of-memory issue, which is mainly due to the huge memory consumption of storing optimizer states and model weights since Megatron does not provide FSDP implementation and optimizer offloading. DeepSpeed-Ulysses has less memory consumption since it has FSDP and optimizer offloading, but still performs worse than LoongTrain-USP and BurstEngine because it can not overlap all-to-all communication with computation. LoongTrain-DoubleRing demonstrates superior throughput performance compared to DeepSpeed-Ulysses, leveraging its advanced capability to overlap communication with computation. However, it still underperforms compared to LoongTrain-USP, primarily because of the significant communication cost that remains unoptimized.
Though LoongTrain-USP has higher training throughput than other baselines, it still suffers from the communication cost of Head Parallelism and RingAttention, respectively. BurstEngine achieves the best training efficiency (TGS and MFU), showing the efficiency of backward communication optimization and topology-aware ring communication with fine-grained overlap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Memory Performance.</span> In terms of memory performance, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 4.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ BurstEngine: an Efficient Distributed Framework for Training Transformers on Extremely Long Sequences of over 1M Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, BurstEngine exhibits the lowest peak memory usage. The figure highlights that DeepSpeed-Ulysses, Loong-Train-DoubleRing, and LoongTrain-USP exhibit comparable memory consumption under the setting (7B model, 32<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800), while BurstEngine saves <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>26.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> memory compared to the best baseline. Under the setting (14B model, 32<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800), DeepSpeed-Ulysses encounters an out-of-memory error due to its limitation on the number of model heads. LoongTrain-DoubleRing and LoongTrain-USP suffer from high memory consumption, which is mainly due to storing the outputs of the LM head. Under this setting, BurstEngine saves <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> memory compared to the best baseline by adopting sequence-level fusion of LM head and loss function. Under the setting of 64<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800, BurstEngine can support training a 7B model with a 4M
sequence length and a 14B model with a 2M sequence length, which all baseline models fail to achieve. This is because BurstEngine exhibits nearly identical memory usage, indicating that BurstEngine achieves almost linear scaling with the device number along the sequence dimension.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>The ablation study of BurstEngine for using 32<math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800 to train a 14B model on the sequences of 1M tokens.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:651.5pt;height:100.4pt;vertical-align:-47.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-28.3pt,4.4pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
